ceo_bio":"Mr. Francisco J. Leon | CEO, President & Director | Age 48 | Compensation: $3.0M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 2K people. Revenue per employee of $1.5M ranks in the below the sector median among Energy peers. Each employee generates $217K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $16K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 2\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (5/10).","financial_summary":["California Resources Corporatio is a mid-cap energy business with a $3.9B market cap on $3.7B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +11% over the past three years and +4% annually across the full decade, a respectable pace for a business of this size. Operating margins are expanding by +7.3pp year-over-year, which is one of the strongest signals that the moat is deepening.","Returns on capital run at 16% against a 9% cost of capital, a 7-point spread that creates value, though narrow enough that competitive pressure could close it.","The business throws off $543M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 15 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 2.38\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 1.0\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage runs at 5.6\u00d7.","Management returns a meaningful 13.2% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insiders hold 4.9% of the shares."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (1.7) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Carries a 1633% premium 